WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.300
Demand to delivery is a highly complex system of exactly 27 steps,

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.700
stretched across eight different functions.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.300
But here's the wild part.

4
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.400
If you ask five people in your business to name those steps right now,

5
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.500
you'll get five completely different chains.

6
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.300
See, every single step needs an owner, an input, an output,

7
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.300
a lead time, and a failure mode.

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.100
If that isn't written down, nobody knows where the system slipped.

9
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.700
Here are the three places it always breaks down.

10
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.300
Breakpoint one, the forecast handoff.

11
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.900
During the handoff to the supply plan,

12
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.700
numbers just kind of change in the dark.

13
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.400
There are no logs of who changed them or why.

14
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.600
This is exactly where forecast accountability dies.

15
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.500
Breakpoint two, customs holds.

16
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.200
You know, a 98% document accuracy rate at the border

17
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.700
sounds perfectly fine on paper

18
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.500
until you realize that missing 2%

19
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.900
is holding up a physical high value shipping container.

20
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.900
We aren't just talking about a stuck cell in Excel.

21
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.600
Breakpoint three, the retail shelf.

22
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.400
Stock sitting somewhere in the network still isn't sellable.

23
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.300
True availability is only measured right there at the customer's shelf.

24
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.200
Not back in your warehouse.

25
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.400
So what's the fix?

26
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.700
Map the chain.

27
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.100
You cannot fix a process you have never actually mapped out.

28
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.000
You've got to do it step by step using a Rossi matrix

29
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.500
to clearly define those owners,

30
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.200
the inputs and the exact failure modes.

31
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.200
The payoff?

32
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.600
You get a full 27 step operating system

33
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.600
that is entirely live, clickable and fully documented.

34
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.900
This forensic approach comes from Vinayak Bhadani

35
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.400
in demand planning and AI out of Dubai.

36
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.600
So are you ready to stop guessing

37
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.100
and start engineering your supply chain decisions?
